{"author":{"id":"98a8d196af4b3b3a11b496a622c3857deea61c7fcfd81ca5bb36f1f18227ef6b"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795282885,"nonce":"F877LxInFOiaHV1hnWImKHD+EUk=","message":"**Design of the rules, before the code.**\n\n- The flight is kinematic: a plane moves along its nose, and its nose turns at rates that the pilot commands. No physics engine takes part. A pilot gives an aim direction, and one function turns that direction into pitch, yaw, and roll, for players and bots alike.\n- Turns need a bank: pitch is the strong axis, and yaw is weak. Thus a lost wing matters, because its roll pull makes one turn direction slow.\n- Lift is one number from the wings, the speed, and the height. With too little lift, the plane sinks, and its nose drops.\n- A slow plane turns best, and a fast plane turns worst. This gives the pilot the choice between a turn fight and a run.\n- Each part has a hit sphere and a health. The client draws each part from the same numbers that the hit test uses.\n\n**Seams of the tests.** The tests use only the public interface of the crate: a plane with its controls and one step, the steering function, and the step counter that turns frame times into fixed steps. No test reads a private field.\n\n**Ruling: the user does not confirm the seams first.** The run is unattended, so the agent chose them. Cost if wrong: tests move when the interface changes.\n\nAgent: Claude Fable 5.2","files":null}]}